Feature: Create and Utilize the purgo_playground Schema

  The feature aims to outline the creation, integration, and usage of the 'purgo_playground' schema to analyze sales data effectively while ensuring compatibility, security, and comprehensive reporting. The schema must support different data processing needs based on user roles and access levels, ensuring accurate and timely data handling.

  Background:
    Given the following list of contacts is available:
      | Role              | Contact Person       |
      | Project Manager   | Alex Johnson         |
      | Data Architect    | Chris Wong           |
      | Security Officer  | Maria Thomas         |
      | Business Analyst  | Liam Clarke          |

  Scenario Outline: Verify successful integration with existing systems
    Given the purpose of the 'purgo_playground' schema is defined as "<Purpose>"
    And it integrates effectively with the current system "<System>"
    When the schema is implemented
    Then it should process "<Data_Type>" with "<Processing_Steps>"
    And it should output "<Expected_Output>"

    Examples:
      | Purpose         | System          | Data_Type              | Processing_Steps            | Expected_Output              |
      | Sales Analysis  | Data Warehouse  | Transaction Data       | Aggregation, Validation     | Sales Report by Region      |
      | Inventory Check | ERP System      | Product Inventory Data | Standardization, Cleansing  | Stock Status Overview       |

  Scenario: Validate user roles and access control
    Given user roles are defined with the following access levels:
      | Role       | Access Level |
      | Admin      | Full Access  |
      | Sales Lead | Read-Only    |
      | Analyst    | Read-Write   |
    And each role is granted correct schema permissions
    When the users attempt to access data in purgo_playground
    Then the schema should deny unauthorized access attempts from non-privileged users
    And log the attempted breach with a message "Unauthorized access attempt detected"

  Scenario: Process and transform sales data correctly
    Given sample sales data in .csv format is uploaded with correct column headers
    When the system processes the data
    Then each data field should adhere to the following validation rules:
      | Field       | Validation Rule                     |
      | country_cd  | Non-empty, ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code |
      | product_id  | Non-empty, starts with 'P'         |
      | qty_sold    | Positive integer                   |
      | sales_date  | YYYY-MM-DD format                  |
    And if any rule is violated, return error message "<Error_Message>"

    Examples:
      | Field      | Error_Message                                      |
      | country_cd | "Country code must be a valid ISO 3166-1 alpha-2"  |
      | product_id | "Product ID must start with 'P'"                   |
      | qty_sold   | "Quantity sold must be a positive integer"         |
      | sales_date | "Sales date must follow the YYYY-MM-DD format"     |

  Scenario: Ensure accurate reporting from processed data
    Given sales data has been processed without errors
    When the system generates reports
    Then reports must include the following sections:
      | Section           | Detail                            |
      | Total Sales       | Aggregate by country and product  |
      | Top Products      | Products with highest sales       |
      | Sales by Region   | Breakdown by country              |
    And reports should be exported in "<File_Format>"

    Examples:
      | File_Format | 
      | PDF         |
      | Excel       |

  Scenario: Handling data updates efficiently
    Given the data update frequency is set to "daily"
    And updates to schema are transmitted via "<Update_Method>"
    When new sales data is available
    Then data must be appended without redundancy
    And ensure data integrity is maintained for all entries

    Examples:
      | Update_Method  |
      | Data Streams   |
      | Batch Process  |

